  "passage": {
    "title": "How Coffee Changed the World",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Coffee is one of the most popular drinks in the world. Every day, people drink more than two billion cups. According to an old story from Ethiopia, a goat farmer called Kaldi discovered coffee about 1,200 years ago when he noticed that his goats became very lively after eating red berries from a certain bush."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "From Ethiopia, coffee travelled to Arabia, where people first made a drink from roasted beans. By the sixteenth century, coffee houses had opened in cities such as Cairo and Istanbul. These places were more than cafés: people went there to talk, play chess, listen to music and share news. They were sometimes called schools of the wise, because a cup of coffee cost very little and conversation was free."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Coffee reached Europe in the seventeenth century. At first, some religious leaders disliked it and called it dangerous, but a pope tasted it and is said to have enjoyed it. Soon, coffee houses appeared in London, where they were known as penny universities, because for one penny you could buy a cup and listen to educated people discuss science and politics. Several important businesses, including an insurance company, started in London coffee houses."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Today, coffee is grown in more than seventy countries, mostly near the equator. Brazil produces about a third of the world's coffee. Millions of farmers depend on it for their income, but many of them earn very little, because prices change a lot from year to year. Fair-trade schemes try to help by paying farmers a minimum price."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Coffee remains a social drink. In many countries, meeting for coffee is the most common way to start a business conversation or a friendship, just as in those early coffee houses. Few people realise that the habit began with a goat farmer and a handful of berries."
      }
    ]
  },
